.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions provide an API for using the form of Hybrid Public Key
Encryption (HPKE) defined in RFC9180. Understanding the HPKE
specification is likely required before using these APIs.
HPKE is used by various other IETF specifications, including the TLS
Encrypted Client Hello (ECH) specification and others.
.Pp
HPKE is a standardised, highly flexible construct for encrypting "to" a
public key that supports combinations of a key encapsulation method
(KEM), a key derivation function (KDF) and an authenticated encryption
with additional data (AEAD) algorithm, with optional sender
authentication.
.Pp
The sender and a receiver here will generally be using some application
or protocol making use of HPKE. For example, with ECH, the sender will
be a browser and the receiver will be a web server.
.Ss Data Structures
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_SUITE
is a structure that holds identifiers for the algorithms used for KEM,
KDF and AEAD operations.
.Pp
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
is a context that maintains internal state as HPKE operations are
carried out.
Separate
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
objects must be used for the sender and receiver.
Attempting to use a single context for both will result in errors.
.Ss OSSL_HPKE_SUITE Identifiers
The identifiers used by
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_SUITE
are:
.Pp
The KEM identifier
.Em kem_id
is one of the following:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0x10 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KEM_ID_P256
.It 0x11 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KEM_ID_P384
.It 0x12 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KEM_ID_P521
.It 0x20 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KEM_ID_X25519
.It 0x21 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KEM_ID_X448
.El
The KDF identifier
.Em kdf_id
is one of the following:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0x01 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KDF_ID_HKDF_SHA256
.It 0x02 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KDF_ID_HKDF_SHA384
.It 0x03 Sy OSSL_HPKE_KDF_ID_HKDF_SHA512
.El
The AEAD identifier
.Em aead_id
is one of the following:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0x01 Sy OSSL_HPKE_AEAD_ID_AES_GCM_128
.It 0x02 Sy OSSL_HPKE_AEAD_ID_AES_GCM_256
.It 0x03 Sy OSSL_HPKE_AEAD_ID_CHACHA_POLY1305
.It 0xFFFF Sy OSSL_HPKE_AEAD_ID_EXPORTONLY
The last identifier above indicates that AEAD operations are not needed.
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_export
can be used, but
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_open
and
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
will return an error if called with a context using that AEAD
identifier.
.El
.Ss HPKE Modes
HPKE supports the following variants of Authentication using a mode
Identifier:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_BASE Ns , 0x00
Authentication is not used.
.It Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_PSK Ns , 0x01
Authenticates possession of a pre-shared key (PSK).
.It Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_AUTH Ns , 0x02
Authenticates possession of a KEM-based sender private key.
.It Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_PSKAUTH Ns , 0x03
A combination of
.Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_PSK
and
.Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_AUTH .
Both the PSK and the senders authentication public/private must be
supplied before the encapsulation/decapsulation operation will work.
.El
.Pp
For further information related to authentication see
.Sx Pre-Shared Key HPKE
modes
and
.Sx Sender-authenticated HPKE Modes .
.Ss HPKE Roles
HPKE contexts have a role - either sender or receiver.
This is used to control which functions can be called and so that
senders do not reuse a key and nonce with different plaintexts.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_free ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_export ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_psk ,
and
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_get_seq
can be called regardless of role.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy OSSL_HPKE_ROLE_SENDER Ns , 0
An
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
with this role can be used with
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_ikme
and
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_authpriv .
.It Sy OSSL_HPKE_ROLE_RECEIVER Ns , 1
An
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
with this role can be used with
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_decap ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_open ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_authpub
and
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set_seq .
.El
.Pp
Calling a function with an incorrect role set on
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
will result in an error.
.Ss Parameter Size Limits
In order to improve interoperability, RFC9180, section 7.2.1 suggests a
RECOMMENDED maximum size of 64 octets for various input parameters.
In this implementation we apply a limit of 66 octets for the
.Fa ikmlen ,
.Fa psklen ,
and
.Fa labellen
parameters, and for the length of the string
.Fa pskid
for HPKE functions below.
The constant
.Em OSSL_HPKE_MAX_PARMLEN
is defined as the limit of this value.
(We chose 66 octets so that we can validate all the test vectors present
in RFC9180, Appendix A.)
.Pp
In accordance with RFC9180, section 9.5, we define a constant
.Em OSSL_HPKE_MIN_PSKLEN
with a value of 32 for the minimum length of a pre-shared key, passed in
.Fa psklen .
.Pp
While RFC9180 also RECOMMENDS a 64 octet limit for the
.Fa infolen
parameter, that is not sufficient for TLS Encrypted ClientHello (ECH)
processing, so we enforce a limit of
.Em OSSL_HPKE_MAX_INFOLEN
with a value of 1024 as the limit for the
.Fa infolen
parameter.
.Ss Context Construct/Free
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_new
creates a
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
context object used for subsequent HPKE operations, given a
.Fa mode
(See
.Sx HPKE Modes Ns ),
.Fa suite
(see
.Sx OSSL_HPKE_SUITE Identifiers )
and a
.Fa role
(see
.Sx HPKE Roles Ns ).
The
.Fa libctx
and
.Fa propq
are used when fetching algorithms from providers and may be set to NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_free
frees the
.Fa ctx
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
that was created previously by a call to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_new .
If the argument to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_free
is NULL, nothing is done.
.Ss Sender APIs
A sender's goal is to use HPKE to encrypt using a public key, via use of
a KEM, then a KDF and finally an AEAD. The first step is to encapsulate
(using
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap )
the sender's public value using the recipient's public key,
.Pf ( Fa pub )
and to internally derive secrets.
This produces the encapsulated public value
.Pf ( Fa enc )
to be sent to the recipient in whatever protocol is using HPKE. Having
done the encapsulation step, the sender can then make one or more calls
to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
to encrypt plaintexts using the secret stored within
.Fa ctx .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap
uses the HPKE context
.Fa ctx ,
the recipient public value
.Fa pub
of size
.Fa publen ,
and an optional
.Fa info
parameter of size
.Fa infolen ,
to produce the encapsulated public value
.Fa enc .
On input
.Fa enclen
should contain the maximum size of the
.Fa enc
buffer, and returns the output size.
An error will occur if the input
.Fa enclen
is smaller than the value returned from
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_public_encap_size .
.Fa info
may be used to bind other protocol or application artefacts such as
identifiers.
Generally, the encapsulated public value
.Fa enc
corresponds to a single-use ephemeral private value created as part of
the encapsulation process.
Only a single call to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap
is allowed for a given
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
takes the
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
context
.Fa ctx ,
the plaintext buffer
.Fa pt
of size
.Fa ptlen
and optional additional authenticated data buffer
.Fa aad
of size
.Fa aadlen ,
and returns the ciphertext
.Fa ct
of size
.Fa ctlen .
On input
.Fa ctlen
should contain the maximum size of the
.Fa ct
buffer, and returns the output size.
An error will occur if the input
.Fa ctlen
is smaller than the value returned from
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_public_encap_size .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap
must be called before the
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal .
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
may be called multiple times, with an internal "nonce" being incremented
by one after each call.
.Ss Recipient APIs
Recipients using HPKE require a typically less ephemeral private value
so that the public value can be distributed to potential senders via
whatever protocol is using HPKE. For this reason, recipients will
generally first generate a key pair and will need to manage their
private key value using standard mechanisms outside the scope of this
API. Private keys use normal
.Xr EVP_PKEY 3
pointers so normal private key management mechanisms can be used for the
relevant values.
.Pp
In order to enable encapsulation, the recipient needs to make it's
public value available to the sender.
There is no generic HPKE format defined for that - the relevant
formatting is intended to be defined by the application/protocols that
makes use of HPKE. ECH for example defines an ECHConfig data structure
that combines the public value with other ECH data items.
Normal library functions must therefore be used to extract the public
value in the required format based on the
.Xr EVP_PKEY 3
for the private value.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_keygen
provides a way for recipients to generate a key pair based on the HPKE
.Fa suite
to be used.
It returns a
.Xr EVP_PKEY 3
pointer for the private value
.Fa priv
and a encoded public key
.Fa pub
of size
.Fa publen .
On input
.Fa publen
should contain the maximum size of the
.Fa pub
buffer, and returns the output size.
An error will occur if the input
.Fa publen
is too small.
The
.Fa libctx
and
.Fa propq
are used when fetching algorithms from providers and may be set to NULL.
The HPKE specification also defines a deterministic key generation
scheme where the private value is derived from initial keying material
(IKM), so
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_keygen
also has an option to use that scheme, using the
.Fa ikm
parameter of size
.Fa ikmlen .
If either
.Fa ikm
is NULL or
.Fa ikmlen
is zero, then a randomly generated key for the relevant
.Fa suite
will be produced.
If required
.Fa ikmlen
should be greater than or equal to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_recommended_ikmelen .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_decap
takes as input the sender's encapsulated public value produced by
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap
.Pf ( Fa enc )
and the recipient's
.Xr EVP_PKEY 3
pointer
.Pf ( Em prov Ns ),
and then re-generates the internal secret derived by the sender.
As before, an optional
.Fa info
parameter allows binding that derived secret to other
application/protocol artefacts.
Only a single call to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_decap
is allowed for a given
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_open
is used by the recipient to decrypt the ciphertext
.Fa ct
of size
.Fa ctlen
using the
.Fa ctx
and additional authenticated data
.Fa aad
of size
.Fa aadlen ,
to produce the plaintext
.Fa pt
of size
.Fa ptlen .
On input
.Fa ptlen
should contain the maximum size of the
.Fa pt
buffer, and returns the output size.
A
.Fa pt
buffer that is the same size as the
.Fa ct
buffer will suffice - generally the plaintext output will be a little
smaller than the ciphertext input.
An error will occur if the input
.Fa ptlen
is too small.
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_open
may be called multiple times, but as with
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
there is an internally incrementing nonce value so ciphertexts need to
be presented in the same order as used by the
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal .
See
.Sx Re-sequencing
if you need to process multiple ciphertexts in a different order.
.Ss Exporting Secrets
HPKE defines a way to produce exported secrets for use by the
application.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_export
takes as input the
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX ,
and an application supplied label
.Fa label
of size
.Fa labellen ,
to produce a secret
.Fa secret
of size
.Fa secretlen .
The sender must first call
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap ,
and the receiver must call
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_decap
in order to derive the same shared secret.
.Pp
Multiple calls to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_export
with the same inputs will produce the same secret.
.Em OSSL_HPKE_AEAD_ID_EXPORTONLY
may be used as the
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_SUITE
.Em aead_id
that is passed to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_new
if the user needs to produce a shared secret, but does not wish to
perform HPKE encryption.
.Ss Sender-authenticated HPKE Modes
HPKE defines modes that support KEM-based sender-authentication
.Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_AUTH
and
.Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_PSKAUTH .
This works by binding the sender's authentication private/public values
into the encapsulation and decapsulation operations.
The key used for such modes must also use the same KEM as used for the
overall exchange.
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_keygen
can be used to generate the private value required.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_authpriv
can be used by the sender to set the senders private
.Fa priv
.Vt EVP_PKEY
key into the
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
.Fa ctx
before calling
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_authpub
can be used by the receiver to set the senders encoded pub key
.Fa pub
of size
.Fa publen
into the
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
.Fa ctx
before calling
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_decap .
.Ss Pre-Shared Key HPKE modes
HPKE also defines a symmetric equivalent to the authentication described
above using a pre-shared key (PSK) and a PSK identifier.
PSKs can be used with the
.Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_PSK
and
.Sy OSSL_HPKE_MODE_PSKAUTH
modes.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_psk
sets the PSK identifier
.Fa pskid
string, and PSK buffer
.Fa psk
of size
.Fa psklen
into the
.Fa ctx .
If required this must be called before
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap
or
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_decap .
As per RFC9180, if required, both
.Fa psk
and
.Fa pskid
must be set to non-NULL values.
As PSKs are symmetric the same calls must happen on both sender and
receiver sides.
.Ss Deterministic key generation for senders
Normally the senders ephemeral private key is generated randomly inside
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap
and remains secret.
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_ikme
allows the user to override this behaviour by setting a deterministic
input key material
.Fa ikm
of size
.Fa ikmlen
into the
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_CTX
.Fa ctx .
If required
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_ikme
can optionally be called before
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_encap .
.Fa ikmlen
should be greater than or equal to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_recommended_ikmelen .
.Pp
It is generally undesirable to use
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_ikme ,
since it exposes the relevant secret to the application rather then
preserving it within the library, and is more likely to result in use of
predictable values or values that leak.
.Ss Re-sequencing
Some protocols may have to deal with packet loss while still being able
to decrypt arriving packets later.
We provide a way to set the increment used for the nonce to the next
subsequent call to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_open
(but not to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
as explained below).
The
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set_seq
API can be used for such purposes with the
.Fa seq
parameter value resetting the internal nonce increment to be used for
the next call.
.Pp
A baseline nonce value is established based on the encapsulation or
decapsulation operation and is then incremented by 1 for each call to
seal or open.
(In other words, the first
.Fa seq
increment defaults to zero.)
.Pp
If a caller needs to determine how many calls to seal or open have been
made the
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_get_seq
API can be used to retrieve the increment (in the
.Fa seq
output) that will be used in the next call to seal or open.
That would return 0 before the first call a sender made to
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal
and 1 after that first call.
.Pp
Note that reuse of the same nonce and key with different plaintexts
would be very dangerous and could lead to loss of confidentiality and
integrity.
We therefore only support application control over
.Fa seq
for decryption (i.e.
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_open )
operations.
.Pp
For compatibility with other implementations these
.Fa seq
increments are represented as
.Vt uint64_t .
.Ss Protocol Convenience Functions
Additional convenience APIs allow the caller to access internal details
of local HPKE support and/or algorithms, such as parameter lengths.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_suite_check
checks if a specific
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_SUITE
.Fa suite
is supported locally.
.Pp
To assist with memory allocation,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_ciphertext_size
provides a way for the caller to know by how much ciphertext will be
longer than a plaintext of length
.Fa clearlen .
(AEAD algorithms add a data integrity tag, so there is a small amount of
ciphertext expansion.)
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_public_encap_size
provides a way for senders to know how big the encapsulated public value
will be for a given HPKE
.Fa suite .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_recommended_ikmelen
returns the recommended Input Key Material size (in bytes) for a given
.Fa suite .
This is needed in cases where the same public value needs to be
regenerated by a sender before calling
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_seal .
.Fa ikmlen
should be at least this size.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_grease_value
produces values of the appropriate length for a given
.Fa suite_in
value (or a random value if
.Fa suite_in
is NULL) so that a protocol using HPKE can send so-called GREASE (see
RFC8701) values that are harder to distinguish from a real use of HPKE.
The buffer sizes should be supplied on input.
The output
.Fa enc
value will have an appropriate length for
.Em suite_out
and a random value, and the
.Fa ct
output will be a random value.
The relevant sizes for buffers can be found using
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_ciphertext_size
and
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_public_encap_size .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_str2suite
maps input
.Fa str
strings to an
.Vt OSSL_HPKE_SUITE
object.
The input
.Fa str
should be a comma-separated string with a KEM, KDF and AEAD name in that
order, for example "x25519,hkdf-sha256,aes128gcm".
This can be used by command line tools that accept string form names for
HPKE codepoints.
Valid (case-insensitive) names are: "p-256", "p-384", "p-521", "x25519"
and "x448" for KEM, "hkdf-sha256", "hkdf-sha384" and "hkdf-sha512" for
KDF, and "aes-gcm-128", "aes-gcm-256", "chacha20-poly1305" and
"exporter" for AEAD. String variants of the numbers listed in
.Sx OSSL_HPKE_SUITE Identifiers
can also be used.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_new
returns an OSSL_HPKE_CTX pointer or NULL on error.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_ciphertext_size ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_public_encap_size ,
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_get_recommended_ikmelen
all return a size_t with the relevant value or zero on error.
.Pp
All other functions return 1 for success or zero for error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example demonstrates a minimal round-trip using HPKE.
.Bd -literal
    #include <stddef.h>
    #include <string.h>
    #include <openssl/hpke.h>
    #include <openssl/evp.h>

    /*
     * this is big enough for this example, real code would need different
     * handling
     */
    #define LBUFSIZE 48

    /* Do a round-trip, generating a key, encrypting and decrypting */
    int main(int argc, char **argv)
    {
        int ok = 0;
        int hpke_mode = OSSL_HPKE_MODE_BASE;
        OSSL_HPKE_SUITE hpke_suite = OSSL_HPKE_SUITE_DEFAULT;
        OSSL_HPKE_CTX *sctx = NULL, *rctx = NULL;
        EVP_PKEY *priv = NULL;
        unsigned char pub[LBUFSIZE];
        size_t publen = sizeof(pub);
        unsigned char enc[LBUFSIZE];
        size_t enclen = sizeof(enc);
        unsigned char ct[LBUFSIZE];
        size_t ctlen = sizeof(ct);
        unsigned char clear[LBUFSIZE];
        size_t clearlen = sizeof(clear);
        const unsigned char *pt = "a message not in a bottle";
        size_t ptlen = strlen((char *)pt);
        const unsigned char *info = "Some info";
        size_t infolen = strlen((char *)info);
        unsigned char aad[] = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 };
        size_t aadlen = sizeof(aad);

        /*
         * Generate receiver's key pair.
         * The receiver gives this public key to the sender.
         */
        if (OSSL_HPKE_keygen(hpke_suite, pub, &publen, &priv,
                             NULL, 0, NULL, NULL) != 1)
            goto err;

        /* sender's actions - encrypt data using the receivers public key */
        if ((sctx = OSSL_HPKE_CTX_new(hpke_mode, hpke_suite,
                                      OSSL_HPKE_ROLE_SENDER,
                                      NULL, NULL)) == NULL)
            goto err;
        if (OSSL_HPKE_encap(sctx, enc, &enclen, pub, publen, info, infolen) != 1)
            goto err;
        if (OSSL_HPKE_seal(sctx, ct, &ctlen, aad, aadlen, pt, ptlen) != 1)
            goto err;

        /* receiver's actions - decrypt data using the receivers private key */
        if ((rctx = OSSL_HPKE_CTX_new(hpke_mode, hpke_suite,
                                      OSSL_HPKE_ROLE_RECEIVER,
                                      NULL, NULL)) == NULL)
            goto err;
        if (OSSL_HPKE_decap(rctx, enc, enclen, priv, info, infolen) != 1)
            goto err;
        if (OSSL_HPKE_open(rctx, clear, &clearlen, aad, aadlen, ct, ctlen) != 1)
            goto err;
        ok = 1;
    err:
        /* clean up */
        printf(ok ? "All Good!\en" : "Error!\en");
        OSSL_HPKE_CTX_free(rctx);
        OSSL_HPKE_CTX_free(sctx);
        EVP_PKEY_free(priv);
        return 0;
    }
.Ed
.Sh WARNINGS
Note that the
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set_seq
API could be dangerous - if used with GCM that could lead to
nonce-reuse, which is a known danger.
So avoid that entirely, or be very very careful when using that API.
.Pp
Use of an IKM value for deterministic key generation (via
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_CTX_set1_ikme
or
.Fn OSSL_HPKE_keygen )
creates the potential for leaking keys (or IKM values).
Only use that if really needed and if you understand how keys or IKM
values could be abused.
